00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,010
Lesson 8.2.

2
00:00:02,390 --> 00:00:03,750
Curvature matrices.

3
00:00:04,883 --> 00:00:07,930
The natural gradient
needs a curvature matrix.

4
00:00:08,310 --> 00:00:12,807
The true Fisher information requires
sampling from the model, so in practice

5
00:00:12,807 --> 00:00:16,711
approximations computable with
automatic differentiation are used.

6
00:00:17,091 --> 00:00:20,944
Today we see the two most common
ones, the empirical Fisher and

7
00:00:20,944 --> 00:00:24,920
the generalized Gauss-Newton, and
two digital ways of solving the

8
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:28,161
system: conjugate gradient
and the Woodbury identity.

9
00:00:29,283 --> 00:00:35,330
The empirical Fisher averages the outer
product of per-example gradients: J Jᵀ.

10
00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:41,960
The generalized Gauss-Newton uses the
Jacobian of the network output and the

11
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:47,387
Hessian of the loss with respect
to that output: G = J_f H_L J_fᵀ.

12
00:00:47,767 --> 00:00:52,938
For exponential-family losses, the
GGN coincides with the true Fisher.

13
00:00:53,318 --> 00:00:56,745
The empirical one, in contrast,
can be very different: in a

14
00:00:56,745 --> 00:00:59,882
logistic regression with
nonzero weights, the relative

15
00:00:59,882 --> 00:01:03,192
difference between the two is
two hundred thirty percent.

16
00:01:04,317 --> 00:01:09,813
A crucial detail: with a batch of b examples
and output dimension d_z, the matrix

17
00:01:09,813 --> 00:01:14,495
has rank at most b d_z, much smaller
than the number of parameters N.

18
00:01:14,875 --> 00:01:17,754
In our example, two hundred parameters and

19
00:01:17,754 --> 00:01:20,565
thirty-two examples give rank thirty-two.

20
00:01:20,945 --> 00:01:27,922
The matrix is singular, so it is
damped: one solves (G + λI) g̃ = ∇ℓ.

21
00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:33,089
Conjugate gradient solves the
system using only matrix-vector

22
00:01:33,089 --> 00:01:36,599
products, and the GGN-vector
product is computed with

23
00:01:36,599 --> 00:01:41,102
Jacobian-vector products in O(b N)
time, without forming the matrix.

24
00:01:41,482 --> 00:01:47,181
Since the damped matrix has at most b + 1
distinct eigenvalues, conjugate gradient

25
00:01:47,181 --> 00:01:52,116
converges in few iterations: twenty-one
to twenty-three in the example,

26
00:01:52,496 --> 00:01:54,942
even when the condition
number reaches seven thousand.

27
00:01:55,322 --> 00:01:59,801
Its weak point is that the iterations are
sequential and cannot be parallelized.

28
00:02:00,917 --> 00:02:05,644
The Woodbury identity exploits
the low rank: inverting G +

29
00:02:05,644 --> 00:02:09,964
λI reduces to inverting a
small matrix of size b d_z.

30
00:02:10,344 --> 00:02:12,781
In the example, the result agrees with the

31
00:02:12,781 --> 00:02:15,276
direct solution to ten
to the minus twelve.

32
00:02:15,656 --> 00:02:20,647
The cost is O(b² d_z²
N + b³ d_z³): excellent

33
00:02:20,647 --> 00:02:24,862
if the batch and the output are small,

34
00:02:25,242 --> 00:02:30,019
but intractable when d_z is the size
of a vocabulary, tens of thousands.

35
00:02:31,150 --> 00:02:33,929
This is the table of
Donatella and colleagues.

36
00:02:34,309 --> 00:02:38,501
Adam and stochastic descent
cost O(b N) per iteration.

37
00:02:38,881 --> 00:02:43,680
Direct natural gradient
costs O(N³) and N² memory.

38
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:48,072
With conjugate gradient, c times
the first-order cost, sequential.

39
00:02:48,452 --> 00:02:51,996
With Woodbury, linear
in N but cubic in b d_z.

40
00:02:52,376 --> 00:02:55,963
In the next lesson we will
see that the thermodynamic

41
00:02:55,963 --> 00:02:59,076
version costs O(b d_z
N) plus the analog time.

42
00:03:00,183 --> 00:03:03,643
In practice, prefer the GGN
to the empirical Fisher; use

43
00:03:03,643 --> 00:03:07,102
Woodbury when batch times
output is small, and truncated

44
00:03:07,102 --> 00:03:10,191
conjugate gradient when
there are many parameters.

45
00:03:10,571 --> 00:03:13,992
In lab eleven you will
implement both on MNIST.

46
00:03:15,117 --> 00:03:19,156
Common mistakes: using the empirical
Fisher as if it were the true one.

47
00:03:19,536 --> 00:03:22,985
Forming the full matrix when its
product with a vector suffices.

48
00:03:23,365 --> 00:03:28,491
Running conjugate gradient to full
convergence, when it is truncated on purpose.

49
00:03:28,871 --> 00:03:32,532
And applying Woodbury with an
output the size of a vocabulary.

50
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:38,469
In summary: the GGN is the preferred
curvature and coincides with the Fisher

51
00:03:38,469 --> 00:03:42,590
in the exponential family; the
empirical Fisher can differ a lot.

52
00:03:42,970 --> 00:03:45,635
The matrix has low rank and is damped.

53
00:03:46,015 --> 00:03:51,113
Conjugate gradient and Woodbury solve
it digitally, each with its limit: the

54
00:03:51,113 --> 00:03:55,607
first is sequential, the second
explodes with the output dimension.

55
00:03:56,717 --> 00:03:59,618
To practice: compute the rank of the GGN.

56
00:03:59,998 --> 00:04:01,774
Write the Woodbury formula.

57
00:04:02,154 --> 00:04:05,934
Explain why conjugate gradient
converges in few iterations.

58
00:04:06,314 --> 00:04:10,827
And say when the empirical Fisher and
the GGN coincide in logistic regression.
